And sometimes the bite is answering the question.

“Why are you so defensive?” looks like an invitation to explain yourself. But the moment you start listing reasons, you’ve accepted the premise that you were defensive. The bait was a chance to be understood. The hook was the premise.

Same cast can be generous. “What would make this worth trying?” invites you to imagine conditions you could actually change. A good question can put an overlooked possibility within reach.

That ties back to your speed example. “How fast are we going?” can mean zero right now or 23 mph over the trip. Whoever gets to pick the time window gets to tell a different true story. Sometimes the smartest move is to ask, “Over what stretch?”